#{[17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$"] [17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}{v}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}v}}-\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}1}}_{{\\color{red}v}{\\color{blue}{u}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ and replace it with the simpler $$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ plus some additional terms."] [17592186065649 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186065652] [17592186066211 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186066215] [17592186066267 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186066268] [17592186066510 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186066515] [17592186066748 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186066753] [17592186067210 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186067213] [17592186067216 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186067217] [17592186069036 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nb^{2} - 8=1 \\left(b + 2 \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\left(b - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069138 :question/worked-solution "$25y^2=4$ gives $y^2=4/25$, so $y=\\pm2/5$."] [17592186069139 :question/worked-solution "$z^2=53-3=50$, hence $z=\\pm\\sqrt{50}=\\pm5\\sqrt2$."] [17592186069163 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\np^{2} - \\frac{1}{6}=1 \\left(p + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right) \\left(p - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069173 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n10 p^{2} - \\frac{1}{10}=10 \\left(p - \\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\left(p + \\frac{1}{10}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069222 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{\\left(t - 3\\right) \\left(t + 3\\right)}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nt = \\pm 3.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069263 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n2 x^{2} - 32=2 \\left(x - 4\\right) \\left(x + 4\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069340 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nq^{2} - 25=1 \\left(q + 5\\right) \\left(q - 5\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nq = \\pm 5.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069341 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n9 y^{2} - 16=9 \\left(y - \\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\left(y + \\frac{4}{3}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186069532 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $x^2+x+C>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power."] [17592186075106 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186075111] [17592186077732 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186077735] [17592186077738 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186077739] [17592186077744 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186077748] [17592186078020 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186078022] [17592186078705 :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation is proportional to the difference between the limiting value and the current value. With limiting value $40$ and rate constant $20$, it is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=20(40-y)=800-20y.\n$$\n\nFor $y>40$ this derivative is negative and approaches zero as $y$ approaches $40$."] [17592186079159 :question/worked-solution "Substitute $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ into the equation and divide by the nonzero factor $e^{\\lambda x}$:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+6=(\\lambda-2)(\\lambda-3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=2$ or $\\lambda=3$."] [17592186079160 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda-6=(\\lambda-6)(\\lambda+1).\n$$\n\nThus all solutions are linear combinations of $e^{6x}$ and $e^{-x}$. The option $e^{5x}$ is not such a solution: substitution gives\n\n$$\n(25-25-6)e^{5x}=-6e^{5x}\\ne0.\n$$"] [17592186079161 :question/worked-solution "The exponential trial solution leads to\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2+9\\lambda+18=(\\lambda+6)(\\lambda+3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=-6$ or $\\lambda=-3$."] [17592186079207 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-3\\lambda-10=(\\lambda-5)(\\lambda+2)=0.\n$$\n\nThe two distinct roots are $5$ and $-2$, so the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{-2x}+Be^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary real constants."] [17592186084836 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186084841]}
